    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. There's only one test class (`AcceptanceTest`); the other two files are the base/helper classes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented rejection of a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date, returning **400**, across three well-separated layers:\n\n1. **`ClinicService`** (interface) \u2014 declared `boolean isRegistrationDateInFuture(LocalDate registrationDate)` alongside the existing registration-date methods.\n\n2. **`ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 implemented it as a single-responsibility check that treats a `null` (omitted) date as valid and compares the supplied date against the server date (`LocalDate.now()`, the same \"server date\" notion already used by `resolveRegistrationDate`):\n   ```java\n   public boolean isRegistrationDateInFuture(LocalDate registrationDate) {\n       return registrationDate != null && registrationDate.isAfter(LocalDate.now());\n   }\n   ```\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 validated the *client-supplied* date (before `resolveRegistrationDate` rolls it forward) and returns `400 BAD_REQUEST`, matching the controller's existing pattern of returning status codes for the daily-limit and city-capacity rules:\n   ```java\n   if (this.clinicService.isRegistrationDateInFuture(owner.getRegistrationDate())) {\n       return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);\n   }\n   ```\n\nThe check runs on the raw supplied value so it isn't confused by the business-day roll-forward, and omitted dates still default to the server date as before. `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsFutureDate` (posting `2999-01-01`) and the rest of the suite pass.",
